#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116429 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **strictly increasing** if its $y$-value always rises as we increase the $x$-value. No section of the graph is allowed to be flat.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd868f19387999e329288fd1d492c071d3f0e12c4e9b474e092bc913791d013.png)\n\nOn the other hand, a function $f(x)$ is **increasing** if its $y$-value *never falls* as we increase the $x$-value.\n\n**Watch Out!** Increasing functions, like the one below, are allowed to have flat sections.\n\n![](images/39/39cf4a0a64391114e7b79eed6f039ea4fd0f0772ba2cfb8d4b36c4ab0a5d53b2.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "75e3de1d-9fd6-536a-b21e-7d6434297ce7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1500 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116430 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **strictly decreasing** if its $y$-value always *falls* as we increase the $x$-value. No section of the graph is allowed to be flat.\n\n![](images/b8/b8a2d54cedc84f159a61bfccc11bdab9adc73eb54b694dd1167802998f4d39d3.png)\n\nOn the other hand, a function $f(x)$ is **decreasing** if its $y$-value *never rises* as we increase the $x$-value. Decreasing functions are allowed to have flat sections.\n\n![](images/4f/4f91b34a57ac42ba82e937c78c74bcb26677e1d6fbf8ed40865f6db73b1cf613.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "81ea8002-60eb-5227-bbdd-bc329f9f3f89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1501 :tutorial/title "Decreasing and Strictly Decreasing Functions"}]}
